{"candidates":[{"content":"The reliability trade-off in complex tasks depends on the nature of the error mode: it reduces to a choice between 'omission' (solved by checklists) and 'misinterpretation' (solved by expert intuition). This relation integrates SRC_004 and SRC_005 by proposing that reliability is a function of the error-type distribution; it is refuted if a task with high interdependency (SRC_005) is found to be more reliably solved by a checklist-led panel than by an expert, provided the failure mode is systemic rather than omissive.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"e14a04ef4dfba975e843434ff347370e5934dc77cdca8665d10824853fa3815e"},{"role":"dependence","target":"dc239e37c194aa5815b9872583807ea9f78314d61589f460919087c8af2b5bc6"}],"typicality":0.9},{"content":"The 'checklist effect' described in SRC_001 shares mechanism with the 'blind spot' for systemic failures in SRC_005, where both represent a cognitive narrowing (atomization) that suppresses the expert's ability to perceive holistic patterns. This relation is refuted if an increase in checklist granularity is shown to restore 'edge-case' sensitivity rather than further masking it.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0c70e2c3455ea22ed37a9975fd933f879fcff71a0cc10d378afc59a66187c058"},{"role":"dependence","target":"dc239e37c194aa5815b9872583807ea9f78314d61589f460919087c8af2b5bc6"}],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"The reliability gain from 'inter-rater convergence' in SRC_002 is compatible with the 'externalized memory' mechanism in SRC_004, as both shift the reliability bottleneck from individual cognitive recall to a shared procedural framework. This relation is refuted if convergence on counter-intuitive variables (SRC_002) occurs without the use of the checklist's externalized prompts (SRC_004).","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"bf9ed458939659b56b354ecd49930feb890961c3d87fc52df6385dac48a8bcd2"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e14a04ef4dfba975e843434ff347370e5934dc77cdca8665d10824853fa3815e"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"The claim in SRC_003 that intuition is superior for high-interdependency tasks contradicts the premise of SRC_002 if the 'trap' variables in SRC_002 are themselves interdependent; essentially, if the checklist can neutralize biases in complex patterns, the 'gestalt' advantage of the expert is diminished. This relation is refuted if checklists are proven incapable of guiding non-experts through non-linear variable interactions.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"bf9ed458939659b56b354ecd49930feb890961c3d87fc52df6385dac48a8bcd2"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3beab44ff5f1406864aee497040c19d810a43ddbee959200e8675fe8cad84626"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"The failure of the panel in SRC_001 inherits the systemic vulnerability described in SRC_003, where the decomposition of a problem into linear steps (checklist) inherently excludes the non-linear integration required for expert intuition. This relation is refuted if a checklist-led panel can identify an edge-case anomaly (SRC_001) that requires the integration of non-linear variables (SRC_003).","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0c70e2c3455ea22ed37a9975fd933f879fcff71a0cc10d378afc59a66187c058"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3beab44ff5f1406864aee497040c19d810a43ddbee959200e8675fe8cad84626"}],"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}